# ==========================================================================
#  O31  Disjoint at the top, sparse in the middle, no inverses
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    The top-level kinds are declared pairwise disjoint, domains and ranges
#    are declared only where every use will agree, and inverses are not
#    declared unless a lesson needs one.
#
#  WHY
#    gist's README lists its structural habits in one breath: sparse domain
#    and range specifications for broader applicability, no inverse
#    properties, and extensive fine-grained disjointness at the highest
#    level. The three go together and each is a bet about the future.
#
#    Disjointness at the top is the cheap axiom that catches the expensive
#    mistake. Nothing is both a place and a bookshop, and saying so lets a
#    reasoner notice when a data load types one thing as both. The Trail
#    declares its ten top-level kinds disjoint in one
#    owl:AllDisjointClasses, and four subkinds of Place disjoint in
#    another. Below that it stops: Author and TrailMember are roles and a
#    shop can be both a member and a shop, so disjointness lower down is
#    where the tenth rule from o29 starts to bite.
#
#    Sparse domains and ranges are the opposite bet. A domain is an
#    inference rule: whatever has this property is of this class. State
#    bs:locatedIn's domain as Bookshop and every publisher with an address
#    becomes a bookshop; the Trail states a union instead, and leaves
#    bs:claimedBy with no domain at all because its subject is a statement.
#    gist goes further and leaves most domains open, on the principle that
#    a property is more reusable than any class it was first used with.
#
#    No inverses is the bet that one direction is enough. Every inverse
#    doubles the query paths, the data, the shapes and the reasoner's work,
#    and buys nothing a SPARQL caret or a SHACL inverse path does not. gist
#    removed all of them in version 12 and picks the direction with fewer
#    results, child to parent. The Trail keeps two on purpose (o30).
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       disjoint at the top      [] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ; owl:members ( Place Bookshop Person Publisher Work ... )
#                                cheap; catches a thing typed as two kinds at once
#
#       sparse in the middle     bs:locatedIn  rdfs:domain [ unionOf ( Bookshop Publisher ) ]
#                                bs:claimedBy  no domain: its subject is a statement
#                                a domain is an inference rule, and a narrow one retypes things
#
#       no inverses              isPartOf, not hasPart; ^bs:imprintOf in SPARQL, sh:inversePath in SHACL
#                                one direction, fewer results, half the paths
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - Declare the top-level kinds disjoint. It is one axiom and it
#      catches the worst data error there is.
#    - A domain is an inference rule. Declare one only when every use of
#      the property will agree with it, and use a union when two will
#      not.
#    - One direction per relationship. The query language and the shapes
#      language both walk it backwards for free.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F05-principles%2Fo31-disjoint-at-the-top-sparse-in-the-middle-no-inverses.ttl
#  SOURCES   gist Style Guide: inverses, and why gist has none
#            https://github.com/semanticarts/gist/blob/develop/docs/gistStyleGuide.md#inverses
#            OWL 2 Primer 4.3 Class Disjointness
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-primer/#Class_Disjointness
#  MEASURED  parses, 45 triples; 7 classes, 3 object properties, 1 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; HermiT: INCONSISTENT
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

# Borrowed annotation properties, declared so the file is OWL 2 DL on its own.
skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:scopeNote      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .
dct:source          a owl:AnnotationProperty .

bs:Place a owl:Class . bs:Bookshop a owl:Class . bs:Person a owl:Class .
bs:Publisher a owl:Class . bs:Work a owl:Class . bs:Event a owl:Class .
[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ; owl:members ( bs:Place bs:Bookshop bs:Person bs:Publisher bs:Work bs:Event ) .

bs:locatedIn
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain  [ a owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( bs:Bookshop bs:Publisher ) ] ;
    rdfs:range   bs:Place ;
    skos:scopeNote "Domain is a union: shops and publishers both have one. A domain of Bookshop alone would retype every publisher."@en .

bs:claimedBy
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range   bs:Source ;
    skos:scopeNote "No domain. The subject is a claim about a statement, which no class here describes."@en .
bs:Source  a owl:Class .

bs:imprintOf
    a            owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain  bs:Publisher ;
    rdfs:range   bs:Publisher ;
    skos:scopeNote "Child to parent, the direction with fewer results. No hasImprint declared here; ask for ^bs:imprintOf."@en .

# What the disjointness catches: one thing typed as two kinds.
bt:oddity  a bs:Place, bs:Bookshop .
